/// \defgroup grp_w1020_debug_cmd_stream_alloc Debug commands, allocator and clipboard helper
/// \ingroup grp_common_services
/// \brief The debug library's built-in `debug` command group, its memory
///        helpers and the clipboard helper behind its dialogs' copy buttons.
///
/// These complete the EA debug library described in \ref grp_w1_debug_lib.
///
/// - **The `debug` command group.** DebugCmdInterfaceDebug and its
///   DebugCmdInterfaceDebug::Execute implement the commands that configure
///   the library at run time: which output classes are active, which report
///   sites are switched off, and options such as time stamps, full-memory
///   crash dumps and the `errors.txt` log.
/// - **The allocator.** DebugAllocMemory() and DebugReAllocMemory() take
///   memory from the Win32 global heap and report a failure as a fatal
///   crash; DebugFreeMemory() in \ref grp_w1_debug_lib releases it.
/// - **The clipboard helper.** DebugCopyToClipboard() copies a report to the
///   Windows clipboard for the copy buttons of the crash and exception
///   dialogs.
///
/// The `Debug` stream operators for integers, `bool` and 64-bit integers are
/// documented in \ref grp_w1012_wwdebug_stream_hooks, and the string,
/// pointer, floating-point, HRESULT and memory-dump operators in
/// \ref grp_w1_debug_lib.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Nothing here reads game data or takes part in the logic simulation, saves
/// or replays. The `debug` commands can be run from the start-up `.dbgcmd`
/// file; see DebugCmdInterfaceDebug::Execute.
///
/// \see \ref grp_w1_debug_lib, \ref sub_common_services_debug


/// \class DebugCmdInterfaceDebug
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_debug_cmd_stream_alloc
/// \brief The debug library's built-in `debug` command group: commands that
///        choose output classes, switch report sites on and off, and set
///        the library's own options.
///
/// Debug commands have the form `group.command arguments`. Sources include
/// the start-up command file, lines typed into an output class that reads
/// input, such as the `con` console (see \ref grp_w1_debug_lib), and code
/// that calls Debug::Command (the profiler, for example, issues
/// `profile.result file_csv` through it). A line that starts with a frame
/// number and a colon (`<frame>:`) is not run at once: Debug::ExecCommand
/// hands it to Debug::ScheduleFrameCommand to queue, and
/// Debug::ExecuteFrameCommands, when called with that frame number or a
/// later one, runs it. Both names are inferred from this behaviour; no
/// original names survive, and which frame counter drives the second is not
/// established here.
/// Debug::ExecCommand splits each line into words and offers the command to
/// every handler registered under the group's name until one of them
/// returns true (a bare `help` goes to all of them). This class is
/// the handler of the `debug` group, and DebugCmdInterfaceDebug::Execute
/// holds all of the group's commands.
///
/// \par Lifetime and ownership
/// Debug::PostStaticInit creates the one instance in memory from
/// DebugAllocMemory() and registers it under the name `debug` with
/// `Debug::AddCommands`; from then on the `Debug` object keeps it in its
/// list of command groups. The constructor only installs the object's
/// virtual function table: the class has no data of its own. Zero Hour:
/// when the library shuts down, the `Debug` object destroys every
/// registered command group through the interface's `Delete`, which runs
/// the destructor and returns the memory with DebugFreeMemory(). BFME 2's
/// Debug::StaticExit no longer has that loop: it deletes the active output
/// classes but no command group, so the `debug` command group stays
/// allocated until the process exits.
///
/// \par How it works on Debug
/// Zero Hour's `Debug` declares the class a friend, and its commands work
/// directly on the object's internals:
/// the list of output classes, the list of command groups, the table of
/// report sites seen so far, the known log groups, the list of patterns that
/// switch report sites on and off, and the option flags.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The group's commands configure the debug library without code changes,
/// for example from the start-up `.dbgcmd` file. See
/// DebugCmdInterfaceDebug::Execute for the commands.
///
/// \see Debug, DebugIOCon::Execute


/// \fn bool DebugCmdInterfaceDebug::Execute(class Debug &dbg, const char *cmd, CommandMode cmdmode, unsigned argn, const char *const *argv)
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_debug_cmd_stream_alloc
/// \brief Runs one command of the `debug` group and writes its reply into
///        the command's report.
///
/// Debug::ExecCommand calls it with the command word that follows the group
/// prefix, so `debug.io con add` arrives as `io` with the arguments `con`
/// and `add`, and with the reply already started: whatever the handler
/// streams into `dbg` becomes the reply that the active output classes
/// receive. A command line that starts with `!` runs in structured mode,
/// meant for tools: replies are short values instead of sentences, and
/// `help` writes nothing.
///
/// | Command | Arguments | Effect |
/// |---|---|---|
/// | `help` | optional command name | lists the group's commands, or explains one |
/// | `list` | `g`, `l`, `d`, `a` or `c`, then an optional pattern | lists the command groups, the log groups seen so far, the log groups that have a description, the assert sites hit so far, or the crash sites hit so far |
/// | `io` | none, `?`, or an output class name and a command for it | shows the active or all output classes, adds or removes one, or passes a command to it |
/// | `alwaysflush` | optional `+` or `-` | asks output classes to flush after every report |
/// | `skipstackwalk` | optional `+` or `-` | leaves stack walks out of non-fatal assert and crash reports; fatal reports and exception reports always include one |
/// | `timestamp` | optional `+` or `-` | starts each report line with the local time |
/// | `windowed` | optional `+` or `-` | sets the library's windowed answer to yes or no |
/// | `fulldump` | optional `+` or `-` | makes crash minidumps full-memory dumps |
/// | `errors` | optional `+` or `-` | also writes non-fatal error reports to `errors.txt` |
/// | `clear`, `add`, `view` | list letters; for `add` also `+` or `-` and a pattern | edits or shows the patterns that switch report sites on and off |
/// | `beep` | none | sounds a beep |
/// | `exit` | none | ends the process through the C runtime's `exit` with code 0, so `atexit` handlers such as Debug::StaticExit still run |
/// | `crash`, `exception`, `fatalcrash` | none | deliberate test crashes, not listed by `help` |
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Command words are compared case-sensitively; only `skipstackwalk` is
///   also accepted as `skipStackWalk`.
/// - The six option commands work alike. An argument starting with `+`
///   turns the option on, one starting with `-` turns it off, and anything
///   else or nothing leaves it. The reply is the current state: text such as
///   `Timestamp: on` in normal mode, `1` or `0` in structured mode.
/// - `windowed` sets the library's answer to whether the game runs in a
///   window: `+` sets it to yes and `-` to no. The setting has three
///   states: set on, set off, or not yet decided, for which normal mode
///   replies `unknown`. These commands are not the only way it gets a value.
///   The library's own window check stores its result in the same setting
///   the first time it finds the game's window. Very likely the game also
///   sets it at start-up: before creating its main window, BFME 2's window
///   set-up (`initializeAppWindows`) passes its windowed choice to a `Debug`
///   virtual, and the body that most probably fills that slot stores yes or
///   no in this same setting. That identification is inferred (the body
///   sits directly after the window check and is reached through a virtual
///   table), not proven, and no original name survives for it. If it
///   holds, `unknown` normally ends when the game window is created, and a
///   `debug.windowed` line in the start-up
///   `.dbgcmd` file, which runs during static initialisation before that
///   window exists, can be overridden there. The structured reply is `1`
///   whenever a value is set, by whichever code, and `0` only while it is
///   undecided.
/// - `list` matches each name against the pattern with Debug::SimpleMatch;
///   the default pattern `*` matches everything. Assert and crash sites are
///   matched and printed as the source file followed by the line in
///   parentheses, with their hit count and `[off]` for a site that is
///   switched off.
/// - `io` with a class name looks the class up among the registered ones.
///   `add` creates it through its factory and refuses a class that is
///   already active; `remove` deletes it. After `add`, and for any other
///   sub-command, the sub-command and the arguments after it go on to the
///   class's own `Execute`, which is how `debug.io con add 120 50` both
///   opens and sizes the console (DebugIOCon::Execute). With no sub-command,
///   or any sub-command other than `add` and `remove`, a class that has not
///   been added gets the reply `Add I/O class first`; `remove` on such a
///   class does nothing and writes no reply.
/// - `clear`, `add` and `view` read their first argument as a set of list
///   letters: `l` logs, `a` asserts, `c` crashes. Other letters are ignored,
///   and an argument with none of them selects all three lists. `add`
///   appends an entry that switches matching sites on (`+`) or off (anything
///   else) and needs all three arguments; `clear` removes the selected
///   lists' entries whose pattern text matches its second argument, all of
///   them by default; `view` prints the selected entries. Patterns may use
///   `*` as a wildcard. A site is named as `list` prints it for asserts
///   and crashes, and by its group name for logs; asserts and crashes start
///   switched on and logs off, and the last matching entry decides. When
///   `add` or `clear` changed the list, every recorded site's state is reset
///   to unknown, so that the patterns are applied to it afresh.
///   Switching crash sites off (for example `debug.add c - *`) also
///   switches off fatal crash reports from those sites, including
///   `fatalcrash` and the out-of-memory reports of DebugAllocMemory() and
///   DebugReAllocMemory(): no report is written, `fatalcrash` does
///   nothing, DebugAllocMemory() returns null and DebugReAllocMemory() goes
///   on to fault.
/// - `crash` writes through a null pointer, raising an access violation.
///   `exception` throws a C++ exception (the thrown value is a pointer to
///   `dbg`), which this function does not catch. `fatalcrash` writes the
///   crash report "Serious test crash. Everything is fine." through the
///   global pointer to the `Debug` object in the fatal mode, which shows
///   the "Game crash" box and ends the game, unless crash reports from that site have been
///   switched off with `debug.add c -`, in which case nothing happens.
/// - `exit` calls the C runtime's `exit` with code 0. The help text says
///   "Exits program immediately.", but `exit` still runs the `atexit`
///   handlers. The library's start-up code registers Debug::StaticExit
///   there; when the last reference to the `Debug` object goes, it flushes
///   the open report, deletes the active output classes and, with
///   `debug.errors` on, opens `errors.txt` in the Windows shell.
/// - `beep` calls the Win32 `Beep` at 1000 Hz for half a second.
///
/// \param dbg     The Debug object. Replies are streamed into it, and the
///                commands read and change its internals.
/// \param cmd     The command word, without the group prefix.
/// \param cmdmode `Normal` for a reply in sentences, `Structured` for a
///                short reply meant for tools.
/// \param argn    Number of arguments.
/// \param argv    The arguments, one word each; Debug::ExecCommand keeps a
///                quoted argument together as one word.
/// \return true when the command belongs to this group, even when its
///         arguments were wrong: an unknown output class or list letter
///         gets a message, not false. In normal mode, false for an unknown
///         command and for `help` about any command that has no help text
///         here: an unknown command, but also the three test crashes, the
///         `skipStackWalk` spelling and `help` itself. Debug::ExecCommand
///         then goes on to offer it to other handlers of the group and,
///         when none of them returns true either, replies `Unknown command`
///         (or, for `help` with an argument,
///         `Unknown command, help not available`, so
///         `debug.help crash` gets that reply although `crash` is a command
///         of this group). In structured mode every
///         `help`, with or without an argument and whether or not the
///         command it names is known, returns true without writing
///         anything, so Debug::ExecCommand stops offering a `help` with an
///         argument at this handler; only an unknown command returns false
///         there, and in that mode Debug::ExecCommand writes no
///         `Unknown command` reply.
///
/// \note Compared with Zero Hour's debug_cmd.cpp and BFME 1's version in
///       Open-BFME-1:
///       - BFME 1 already has `skipstackwalk`, `windowed`, `fulldump` and
///         `errors`, and its `a` and `c` lists are asserts and crashes.
///         Zero Hour has none of the four, and its lists are asserts with
///         crashes (`a`) and checks (`c`).
///       - `beep` and the three test crashes are in neither.
///       - `exit` uses exit code 0, where both use 1.
/// \note Some help texts disagree with the code. `errors` claims to be on by
///       default and `skipstackwalk` to be off, but the `Debug` constructor
///       turns `errors` off, and turns `skipstackwalk` on when a debugger is
///       attached (DebugIsDebuggerAttached(), a descriptive name; the
///       original is not known).
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Apart from the commands that end the process, none of these touches
///   game state, saves or replays; they change only what the debug library
///   reports and where. Put them in the
///   start-up `.dbgcmd` file, one per line (see \ref grp_w1_debug_lib), or
///   type them into the console once `debug.io con add` has opened it.
/// - For crash reports from a mod: every exception report also writes a
///   minidump file into the current directory, named after the game
///   version, the date and time, the build identifier and the process and
///   thread ids, when the Windows debug help library can write one.
///   `debug.fulldump +` makes it a full-memory dump; otherwise it is the
///   smaller kind, which adds the modules' data segments but not the rest
///   of memory. `debug.errors +` keeps the non-fatal error reports in
///   `errors.txt`.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// `beep` uses the Win32 `Beep`, the minidumps the Windows debug help
/// library, and `crash` relies on a hardware access violation reaching the
/// library's exception handling. A port can keep the command set and
/// replace those three seams.
///
/// \see Debug::ExecCommand, Debug::AddPatternEntry, DebugIOCon::Execute


/// \file DebugAllocMemory.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_debug_cmd_stream_alloc
/// \brief The debug library's allocation helper, DebugAllocMemory().


/// \file DebugReAllocMemory.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_debug_cmd_stream_alloc
/// \brief The debug library's resize helper, DebugReAllocMemory().


/// \file WWDebug/DebugCopyToClipboard.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_debug_cmd_stream_alloc
/// \brief The helper behind the debug dialogs' copy buttons,
///        DebugCopyToClipboard().


/// \fn void *DebugAllocMemory(unsigned int numBytes)
/// \brief Allocates a block for the debug library from the Win32 global
///        heap; failure is a fatal crash.
///
/// The debug library's own allocator. Taking memory straight from Windows
/// keeps the library independent of the engine's memory pools. Callers include Debug::PreStaticInit (the
/// `Debug` object itself), Debug::PostStaticInit (the `debug` command
/// group), the lists of report sites, log groups and patterns
/// (Debug::AddFrameEntry, Debug::AddLogGroup, Debug::AddPatternEntry),
/// Debug::ExecCommand (its working copy of a command line), the output
/// classes' factories, and DebugReAllocMemory(). Blocks go back with
/// DebugFreeMemory().
///
/// \par Implementation
/// One `GlobalAlloc` call with no flags: a fixed block, not zeroed, whose
/// handle is its address. If it fails, the function writes the crash report
/// "Debug mem alloc failed" through the global pointer to the `Debug`
/// object in the fatal mode (normally the "Game crash" box, then exit; see
/// the return value for the exception), after recording the report site as release-build crash
/// reports do. The report names no file or line. Zero Hour's body is
/// the same.
///
/// \param numBytes Size of the block in bytes.
/// \return The block. Failure normally does not come back to the caller,
///         because the fatal report ends the game. The exception is a
///         report site whose crash reports have been switched off with
///         `debug.add c -` (see DebugCmdInterfaceDebug::Execute): the report
///         is then skipped and null is returned.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The three helpers (this one, DebugReAllocMemory() and DebugFreeMemory())
/// are the library's general allocator. A port can map them onto its own
/// allocator, keeping the rule that a failed allocation ends the program.
/// Some code still allocates directly: DebugCopyToClipboard(), for example,
/// takes its block from the Win32 global heap itself, because the clipboard
/// takes ownership of it.
///
/// \see DebugReAllocMemory, DebugFreeMemory


/// \fn void *DebugReAllocMemory(void *oldPtr, unsigned int newSize)
/// \brief Resizes a block from the debug library's allocator, with
///        `realloc`-like rules for a null pointer and a zero size.
///
/// Callers include Debug::AddOutput (the report buffers), Debug::Update and
/// BFME 2's Debug::GetInput (the buffers that collect typed command input;
/// Zero Hour has no such function, and the name GetInput, carried over from
/// BFME 1, has no recorded source, so treat it as provisional) and
/// DebugExceptionhandler::ExceptionFilter (its copy of the exception report
/// text).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - A null `oldPtr` gets a new block from DebugAllocMemory(), or null when
///   `newSize` is 0.
/// - A `newSize` of 0 frees the block and returns null.
/// - Otherwise `GlobalReAlloc` is tried first. It is called without the flag
///   that lets a fixed block move, so growing fails whenever the block cannot
///   grow where it is. The function then allocates a new block, copies the
///   smaller of the old block's real size (from `GlobalSize`) and the new
///   size, and frees the old block.
/// - If that new block cannot be allocated, the function writes the crash
///   report "Debug mem realloc failed" in the fatal mode, as
///   DebugAllocMemory() does, unless crash reports from that site have
///   been switched off with `debug.add c -`. In that case the report is
///   skipped and the function goes on to copy into the null pointer, which
///   faults.
/// - Bytes added by growing are not zeroed.
///
/// Zero Hour's body is the same.
///
/// \param oldPtr  Block to resize, from DebugAllocMemory() or this function;
///                may be null.
/// \param newSize New size in bytes; 0 frees the block.
/// \return The resized block, which may have moved, so the old pointer must
///         not be used again; null when the block was freed or nothing was
///         allocated.
///
/// \see DebugAllocMemory, DebugFreeMemory


/// \fn void DebugCopyToClipboard(const char *text)
/// \brief Puts a copy of a text on the Windows clipboard as plain text.
///
/// The copy buttons of the debug library's dialogs use it, so that a player
/// can paste a report into a bug report: ExceptionDlgProc copies the
/// exception report text, and the assert and crash dialog's copy button
/// copies the dialog's title and message followed by the game's version and
/// build strings. Zero Hour's debug library has no clipboard code. The name
/// is descriptive; the original name is not known.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// It does nothing for a null text or when the clipboard cannot be opened.
/// Otherwise it copies the text and its terminator into a new moveable,
/// zero-filled, shareable global memory block, empties the clipboard and
/// hands the block over in the `CF_TEXT` format, after which the clipboard
/// owns it. If the block cannot be allocated, the clipboard is closed
/// unchanged. The clipboard is opened without an owner window.
///
/// \param text Terminated text to copy; null is allowed and does nothing.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Win32 clipboard API with 8-bit `CF_TEXT` text. A port would use its
/// platform's clipboard; the report text it copies is plain English.
///
/// \see DebugExceptionhandler::ExceptionFilter
